// Lesson 4.3 — Chase: The Aggro Radius
// SOLO BUILD (scaffold level 4) — like 3.2 it hands over no code and no API
// table. Every part has been built once already: the radius is 3.1's trigger,
// the two callbacks that answer it are 2.1's, and moving toward a point is
// 4.1's single MoveTowards line. The one genuinely new part is the BRANCH: the
// chase does not get its own movement code, it gets its own DESTINATION.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (read from the teacher's project):
//   Assets/Prefabs/MobileEnemy.prefab
//       root  tag Enemy, CapsuleCollider2D, Rigidbody2D Dynamic gravity 0
//       child "Detection Radius"  CircleCollider2D m_IsTrigger: 1, radius 5
//   Assets/Scripts/EnemyController.cs — the built chase:
//       bool inRange = false;  Transform target;
//       Update():
//           if (inRange) { targetPosition = target.position; }
//           else { ...the 4.1 arrival/advance...; targetPosition = patrolPoints[patrolNumber].position; }
//           float step = speed * Time.deltaTime;
//           transform.position = Vector3.MoveTowards(transform.position, targetPosition, step);
//       OnTriggerEnter2D: CompareTag("Player") -> inRange = true; target = other.gameObject.transform;
//       OnTriggerExit2D:  CompareTag("Player") -> inRange = false; target = null;
//
// THE OLD PAGE TAUGHT A DIFFERENT MECHANISM: a serialized float aggro radius, a
// Vector3.Distance() check every frame, GameObject.FindWithTag(), and a
// normalised direction vector the student had to derive. The build does none of
// it — the radius is a collider, and Vector3.MoveTowards() computes the
// direction for every target. The lesson's centre of gravity moves with it:
// the hard part is no longer the maths, it is choosing the destination so that
// exactly ONE movement line writes the transform.
//
// There is no enemy animator call in EnemyController, so the old "face the way
// it is chasing" part is gone. Do not reintroduce it without a build to match.
//
// The parts below are NOT rendered at level 4 (see src/lib/decompose.ts) —
// they are the record of the intended split.
//
// Answer-box ids are positional and persisted: the socratic block keeps its
// three questions in order, and the decompose block keeps its one turn.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'An enemy that comes for me when I get close. That is the whole feature, and like every ' +
    'feature in this course it is not one problem — it is several standing in a line, one of ' +
    'which you have never solved before.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Give the enemy a circle that notices the player',
      does:
        'The Detection Radius child that is already on the MobileEnemy prefab: a Circle Collider 2D with Is Trigger ticked. The radius is a shape in the scene, so it is visible and per-instance.',
      doc:
        '3.1 — The Turret: the same child collider and the same checkbox, on a different prefab.',
      href: '3.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Remember whether the player is inside it',
      does:
        'One bool, `inRange`, starting false, and one reference, `target`, to the player who walked in. Two fields, and only one of them changes when the player leaves.',
      doc: '3.1 — the `inRange` field and the `target` reference you wrote on the turret.',
      href: '3.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Hear the player arrive, and hear them leave',
      does:
        'OnTriggerEnter2D() and OnTriggerExit2D(), each checking other.gameObject.CompareTag("Player"). Two callbacks, because a circle has two edges.',
      doc: '2.1 — the trigger callbacks, and their exit twin from 3.1.',
      href: '2.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Choose the destination before you move',
      does:
        'One branch: while inRange, the destination is the player\'s position; otherwise it is the patrol point you already picked. The chase is a new answer to "where am I going", not a new movement.',
      doc: 'the destination variable from your own 4.1 patrol.',
      href: '4.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Let the movement line you already wrote do the work',
      does:
        'Vector3.MoveTowards() with the same speed and the same deltaTime. It computes the direction from the two positions on every frame, which is why nothing has to be normalized here.',
      doc: 'the movement line in your own 4.1 patrol.',
      href: '4.1.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Most of the parts inside this feature are things you have already built somewhere else: a trigger and a bool from the turret, a movement toward a target from the guard. Only one part has no earlier version — deciding which place the enemy is walking toward, and guaranteeing that exactly one movement line runs. That is what a well-sequenced course feels like from the inside: the same parts in a new order, with one genuinely new one among them.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: could you look this part up on its own, right now, in one ' +
    'search? "Make the enemy chase me" cannot be looked up. "Do something different while the player is inside a trigger" can. If a part is still too big to search for, it is two parts.',
  turns: [
    'The enemy never gets a second movement line — it gets a second destination. Name the one variable both behaviours write, and say what the player would see if you added a separate chase movement instead.',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>4.3 — Chase: The Aggro Radius</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Patrol is calm. One trigger turns a guard into a hunter — and you built that trigger's answer in
    <a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>. What is new is not the movement: it is choosing which place the
    enemy is walking toward.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I make an enemy chase me?', 4)}

${renderDecompose('4.3', DECOMPOSE, 4)}

<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Scene view →</strong> save the Overworld before you touch anything, then
            <strong>Project window →</strong> <code>Assets/Prefabs/MobileEnemy</code>. All four
            instances share one prefab, so a change to the radius changes all four — but the radius
            is a <em>collider</em>, so an instance can override it. Decide before you write anything.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy → MobileEnemy → Detection Radius</strong>: a
            <strong>Circle Collider 2D</strong> with <strong>Is Trigger</strong> ticked and
            <strong>Radius</strong> <code>5</code>. That is the aggro radius — a shape the player can
            see in the Scene view, not a number buried in a script.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Inspector →</strong> the prefab root: the <code>EnemyController</code> from 4.1,
            with its <strong>Capsule Collider 2D</strong> and <strong>Dynamic Rigidbody 2D</strong>.
            The body is already there; today's code still moves the Transform, and the note at the
            bottom of the code half says what that costs.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view →</strong> press Play and walk near an enemy with the Console open. It
            ignores you and walks its beat. That is the "before" you are about to change.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Once the code half is done — Play again</strong> and cross the ring slowly. Watch
            where the change of direction happens relative to the circle you can see in the Scene
            view: it is the collider, not a distance the enemy measured.
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>No API table: every call today is one you have already made. One file — <code>Assets/Scripts/EnemyController.cs</code>, your patrol from <a href="4.1.html">4.1</a>.</p>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Slow down on one part: choosing the destination.</strong> Every target you have moved toward so far was placed in the scene — a patrol point you wired in <a href="4.1.html">4.1</a>. This time the same movement line has two possible destinations, and a remembered answer decides which one it gets. That is where the bugs will be: not in the arithmetic, but in which branch ran.
        <p>The one fact I will hand you: <code>Vector3.MoveTowards(transform.position, targetPosition, step)</code> already works out the direction from the two positions, every frame. You do not need a subtraction, a <code>.normalized</code> or a velocity — the 4.1 line is the whole chase as long as <code>targetPosition</code> is right.</p>
    </div>

    <p>The second decision is not about maths. Your <code>Update()</code> patrols unconditionally and now has to chase as well — two behaviours that want the same object, with no earlier lesson to copy from. Pick one of these three on purpose:</p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>How the two behaviours coexist</th><th>What the code does each frame</th><th>What the player sees</th><th>The risk</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Chase is an <code>if</code>; patrol is the <code>else</code></strong> — <em>this project</em></td>
            <td>The branch picks a destination; then the one movement line runs, wherever the destination came from.</td>
            <td>A clean switch: the moment you cross the line, the enemy stops walking its beat and comes for you.</td>
            <td>Exactly one writer moves the enemy, so nothing fights — but the branch has to be written in the right order, and the patrol's own index is frozen while the chase runs, not reset.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Chase is an <code>if</code>; the patrol keeps running anyway</strong></td>
            <td>Both call a movement in the same frame, each with its own destination.</td>
            <td>A stutter, or an enemy that drifts toward you while still dutifully stepping through its route.</td>
            <td>Two systems moving one object, and their sum is a third movement <em>nobody designed</em>. With the <a href="4.1.html">4.1</a> route this is easy to miss: the patrol's arrival check still fires, so the index advances underneath the chase and the beat restarts somewhere new.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Chase is an <code>if</code>; the patrol is switched off by a flag</strong></td>
            <td>The flag records the mode, and the patrol checks it.</td>
            <td>Same as the first row, at first.</td>
            <td>One extra field, and one extra thing to keep true — and the moment you have a third behaviour, one more clause in every check. Keep this thought for <a href="4.5.html">4.5</a>.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification for the chase.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which two fields does the trigger write, and which one has to start <code>false</code>? What does <code>target</code> hold — the player's collider, the player's GameObject, or the player's Transform — and which of those does the destination line need?</li>
            <li>What happens on the way <em>out</em> of the circle, and why does the exit callback set <code>target = null</code> rather than leave the old reference lying around? Name the exception the other choice leaves behind.</li>
            <li>Which branch wins when the enemy is inside its patrol beat and inside the aggro radius at the same time? Point at the line that decides it.</li>
            <li>Does the patrol's own memory — its index — reset or freeze while the chase runs? Say what the player sees either way.</li>
            <li>What does the enemy do while chasing that a wall should stop, and what actually stops it? The body is Dynamic and the movement is a Transform write — say what that means in one sentence.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: States, Transitions and Two Writers</h3>
        <ol>
<li>You gave the turret a trigger in 3.1 and it opened fire. What does the enemy do with that same answer that the turret did not do — and why does that make the enemy harder to build?</li>
<li>Your enemy is inside its patrol beat and inside the aggro radius at the same time. Which behaviour must win? Describe exactly what the player sees if you pick the other one.</li>
<li>If both behaviours moved the enemy in one frame, what is the sum of "walk right at 2 units per second" and "walk toward the player at 2 units per second"? Why is that sum not a chase — and how does one shared destination avoid it?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Break the chase into parts, and say in one line what each part does and where you built it before</li>
        <li>Explain why the aggro radius is a collider rather than a number in the script, and what the trigger needs in order to fire at all</li>
        <li>Explain your patrol-to-chase transition, point at the construct that stops both behaviours running at once, and say why two writers on one transform produce motion nobody designed</li>
        <li>Say what <code>target</code> holds, what happens to it on exit, and why <code>null</code> is the right value there</li>
        <li>Design an aggro radius that reads as fair next to the turret's detection range, and say which of the two the player should meet first</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/ask.html">How do I&hellip;? index</a> — find this lesson again by the question you were asking</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/index.html">Unit 4: The Enemy</a> — chase and pursuit in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/triggers-vs-collisions.html">Triggers vs Collisions</a> — what a trigger reports, and when</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — trigger callbacks, <code>CompareTag()</code> and <code>Vector3.MoveTowards()</code></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="7.1.html">7.1 — Tilemaps: The Level Editor</a> — the enemy has a beat; now the cave gets a floor plan.</p>
</div>`;
